    "title": "docs: migrate documentation to GitBook with Web3 aesthetic",
    "body": "Closes #18\n\n## Summary\nMigrates the documentation from mdBook to GitBook (Git Sync) with a Web3-oriented dark/neon design direction, restructured so the problem statement, lint reference, and integration guide are one click from the landing page.\n\n## What changed\n- **Added `.gitbook.yaml`** — GitBook Git Sync config (`root: ./docs/`, landing page `README.md`, nav `SUMMARY.md`).\n- **Ported all mdBook content**: `introduction.md` → `docs/README.md` (problem statement front-and-center, resource-cost table, quick links), `SUMMARY.md` → grouped GitBook table of contents, `integration.md` and the 3 lint pages re-styled with GitBook hint blocks and titled code blocks. All original prose carried over unchanged.\n- **Added `docs/branding.md`** — design spec for the hosted space (neon-teal-on-dark palette with hex values, dark-default theme, fonts, logo guidance) plus in-repo writing conventions and Git Sync cutover steps.\n- **Removed mdBook**: `docs/book.toml`, the old `docs/src/` tree, and `.github/workflows/pages.yml`.\n- **README**: the three lint links now point at the in-repo doc pages instead of the retired GitHub Pages URLs (swap for the GitBook URL once the space is live).\n\n## Key design decisions\n- **GitBook doesn't support custom CSS via Git Sync**, so visual theming must be applied in the hosted space's Customization settings. The issue anticipated this (\"…or design suggestions for the hosted space\"): `docs/branding.md` is that spec, and the in-repo styling uses everything GitBook does allow (hint blocks, grouped nav, titled code blocks).\n- Kept all docs under `docs/` via `root: ./docs/` rather than moving markdown to the repo root.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [x] Migrate existing mdBook content (`docs/src/*`) to GitBook format\n- [x] Dark-mode, high-contrast Web3 aesthetic (in-repo blocks + hosted-space spec in `docs/branding.md`)\n- [x] Integration guide, lint reference, and problem statement highly visible and well-structured\n- [x] Remove old mdBook config (`book.toml`) and the mdBook deploy workflow\n- [x] Branch `docs/gitbook-migration`\n\n## Verification\n- Link check: every relative link in the 5 new pages resolves to an existing file.\n- Content audit: every sentence of the original 7 mdBook pages is preserved.\n- CI: this diff touches no Rust/Cargo files, so `cargo test --workspace` (the `Lint` workflow) is unaffected.\n\n## Cutover note (action needed after merge)\nMerging removes the GitHub Pages deploy, so **no hosted docs are live until a maintainer creates the GitBook space and enables Git Sync** (steps in `docs/branding.md`). If preferred, `pages.yml` removal can be deferred to a follow-up PR instead.\n\n## Follow-ups\n- Enable GitBook Git Sync and apply the branding settings (maintainer, requires GitBook account).\n- Update the repo About link and README lint links to the final GitBook URL.\n\n## Security\nDocs-only change: no code, dependencies, secrets, or CI permissions added; one workflow (Pages deploy) removed.\n",
